From 818fb904ecb47dc6478ce17e36c34d7ea0acdf7d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jun Kawasaki <jun784@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 04:03:18 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] type-system: :principal, and a signature cannot mint one
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The language contract already had `[:cap kind resource]` — an opaque
capability whose presence in a parameter forces the matching effect into the
signature. It had no way to say who an execution runs as, so every consumer
that needed one reached for a string, and a string is a claim.

`:principal` is added as an OPAQUE type, deliberately not a primitive: every
primitive is inhabitable by writing one down, and a principal a program can
write down is a principal it claims rather than one it was given.

Two properties follow, and both are asserted rather than described:

- **A principal is not authority.** `implied-effects` reads `:cap` and
  nothing else, so a `:principal` parameter forces no effect. Being told who
  is calling permits nothing on its own; deciding what may proceed from who
  is calling is the ambient-authority mistake this language exists to not
  make. Identity is for designation and accountability, the capability is
  what permits.
- **A signature cannot produce a principal it was not given.** A return that
  mentions `:principal` while no parameter does is rejected as
  `:principal/minted`, at any depth — `[:option :principal]`,
  `[:result :principal E]`, `[:vector :principal]`, `[:map K :principal]`.
  Wrapping is not laundering. Passing one on is fine, because there the
  principal came in through a parameter. Move gets this from a VM that is the
  only thing able to construct a `signer`; a type contract cannot enforce
  provenance that way, so it enforces the part it can see: the shape of the
  signature.

Recorded in `lang/surface-status.edn` as an `:intentional-security-constraint`
invariant beside `:no-ambient-authority`, and the entry says plainly that
**only the type contract is done**: `:backends #{}`,
`:not-implemented #{:guest-grammar :kir :wasm32 :restricted-esm :native}`.
No backend represents a `:principal` value and the grammar has no way to bind
one. `docs/lang/surface-matrix.md` regenerated — the on-disk-matrix test
caught the staleness, which is that gate working.

Two things this does NOT check, named so the silence is not read as coverage:
that a principal cannot be rendered to `:string` is the absence of any such
operation in the surface, not a rule stated here; and that a runtime hands one
in rather than a program constructing one is a property of the grammar.

Measured: 5 tests / 26 assertions / 0 failures. Full suite 258 -> 263 tests,
1399 -> 1425 assertions, with the identical 5 failures / 3 errors before and
after (all sibling checkouts absent from this environment). Lint 0 errors, no
new warnings.

Each claim was shown to discriminate: unwiring the mint rule reddens 7
assertions, making `mentions-principal?` shallow reddens 6, moving
`:principal` into `primitive-types` reddens the no-literal assertion, and
making a principal parameter imply an effect reddens 7.

